rules.requirement:
For a unit covered by the Rent Ordinance, a landlord may evict only for one of 17 just causes under § 37.9(a), and that cause must be the dominant motive. Expiration of a rental agreement or a change in ownership is not just cause.

The causes are:
- non-payment, habitual late payment or frequent bounced checks;
- failure to cure a substantial breach;
- nuisance or substantial interference;
- illegal use;
- refusal to sign a like extension or renewal;
- refusal of lawful access;
- an unapproved subtenant as the only remaining occupant;
- owner or limited relative move-in;
- sale under an approved condominium conversion;
- demolition or permanent removal from housing use;
- temporary capital improvement work;
- substantial rehabilitation of a building at least 50 years old where the cost is at least 75% of new construction;
- Ellis Act withdrawal of all units;
- lead remediation under Health Code Articles 11 or 26;
- demolition or removal under a Chapter 56 development agreement;
- expiry of a Good Samaritan occupancy agreement, with notice served within 60 days;
- demolition for a development project under Planning Code § 317.

Just cause is also required to remove housing services such as garage, parking, storage and laundry. A landlord who complies with § 37.2(r) may temporarily remove some services for mandatory soft-story seismic retrofit work.

Relocation payments are set by the date the eviction notice is served:
- Owner or relative move-in, demolition or permanent removal, temporary capital improvement and substantial rehabilitation evictions (§ 37.9C): a per-tenant amount, capped per unit, plus an additional amount for each elderly (60+) or disabled tenant or household with minor children.
- Ellis Act evictions (§ 37.9A): a per-tenant amount, capped per unit, plus an additional amount for each elderly (62+) or disabled tenant.
- Temporary capital improvement evictions of less than 20 days: governed by Cal. Civ. Code § 1947.9, not § 37.9C.
